        "problem": "If $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, then ${E}^{4}=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${E}^{2}=E,$ so $E$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${E}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{4} & ={E}^{2}\\cdot {E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & ={E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{E}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
